{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And if you should say, etc.</b> The plain sense of the verse proceeds along these lines: ״וְכִי תֹאמְרוּ מַה נֹּאכַל״ — and since there are in the meaning of this statement two paths: the first is a rationale for negating the desire for this matter, that they should not sow, etc., for if so, what shall we eat, and we must perforce sow to sustain our lives upon the land. The second path is a question about the consequence of fulfilling this commandment — what shall we eat, since we do not sow. Therefore the verse comes and clarifies the intent of the statement ״מַה נֹּאכַל״ by concluding with <b>the assertion הֵן לֹא נִזְרָע</b>, etc. — meaning, it is not that we say ״מַה נֹּאכַל״ to give a rationale for negating the fulfillment of this commandment; rather, we do accept ״לֹא נִזְרָע״, etc. — but like a son who says before his father ״מַה נֹּאכַל״, then ״וְצִוִּיתִי״, etc. But if their intent in the statement ״מַה נֹּאכַל״ were along the first path, then HaShem would not command the blessing upon them."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:01:13.522376964Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}